course: dictionary
lesson: 1942
title: Who is in charge? Meet the commissioner
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A commissioner is an important official. Officials are people who are put in charge of something, like a department, an organisation or an area of work. Look at the clue in this sentence: «In 1839 Commissioner Lin Zexu had more than a thousand tonnes of British opium destroyed.» Someone had to be in charge to make that happen, so a commissioner is someone with the power to make big decisions and give orders.
explain: The word also appears in jobs with names. A police commissioner is the head of a police force, so in a story the police commissioner gives orders to detectives. A county commissioner or a children's commissioner is in charge of an area of work, such as a county's services or children's rights.
explain: Try the swap check. Put «the person in charge» in place of commissioner: «In 1839 the person in charge had more than a thousand tonnes of British opium destroyed.» The sentence still makes sense, so you have found the right meaning. Use the same check with any new word you meet.
fact: A commissioner is an important official put in charge of a government department, an organisation or an area of work.
fact: In some police forces, the commissioner is the head of the police.
fact: In sport, a commissioner can be the official in charge of a whole league.
fact: Useful phrases include police commissioner, children's commissioner, county commissioner and the Commissioner of Police.
fact: Context clue: a commissioner has the power to make decisions and give orders, so look for actions like «had ... destroyed» that show someone is in charge.
exercise: «In 1839 Commissioner Lin Zexu had more than a thousand tonnes of British opium destroyed.» What is a commissioner? | A sports player who scores goals | A person who runs a cake shop | A type of ship | *An important official in charge of an area of work
exercise: In stories, the police commissioner gives orders to whom? | Pupils in a school | *Detectives | Pilots | Shopkeepers
exercise: In sport, what does a commissioner lead? | A petrol station | A cup of tea | *A whole league | A school choir
test: Which phrase names a person in charge of the police? | *Police commissioner | Children's commissioner | Commissioner's cat | County commissioner
test: «Commissioner Lin Zexu had more than a thousand tonnes of British opium destroyed.» Which clue shows that the commissioner was in charge? | He went to the beach | He was very tired | *He had the opium destroyed | He bought a boat
exam: In the sentence «In 1839 Commissioner Lin Zexu had more than a thousand tonnes of British opium destroyed», which meaning of commissioner fits best, and how do you know? | *An important official in charge; because he had the opium destroyed, so he must have had the power to order it | A type of food; because the sentence mentions tonnes | A sports official; because the sentence mentions 1839 | A police officer; because the sentence mentions Britain
